syntax: allocate statements, expansions, quotes and assignments in batches

A parser already takes literals and words from chunks, which keeps a large
script from costing one allocation per node. Statements, parameter expansions,
double-quoted strings and assignments are the next most common nodes and were
still allocated one by one, and each statement list grew its slice from nothing.

They now come from chunks in the same way, and a list collects its statements
on a stack the parser keeps, then copies them to a slice of exactly their size.
The trees are the same, and a chunk's unused nodes stay for the next parse.

Reproducer, before this change: a script of 5000 lines cost about 14000
statement allocations and a further 12000 for growing lists on each parse.

